meta {
  name: Create Bug Report (Too Short)
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/bug-reports
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "description": "broken"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 400 Bad Request", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(400);
  });

  test("names the field rather than failing opaquely", function () {
    const errors = res.getBody().errors;
    expect(errors.fieldErrors).to.have.property("description");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /bug-reports — the minimum-description floor

  Pins the one piece of validation that is a *product* decision rather than a
  type check: a report has to say something. "broken" is six characters and is
  rejected; the floor is ten (`MIN_BUG_DESCRIPTION_LENGTH` in `@sfa/shared`,
  which the widget's submit button also reads, so the client and the API agree
  on the same number).

  The rest of the body is optional — no severity, no screenshots and no context
  is a perfectly valid report, and this request proves that too by omitting all
  three and still failing only on the description.

  ## Response `400`
  ```json
  {
    "message": "Validation failed",
    "errors": {
      "formErrors": [],
      "fieldErrors": {
        "description": ["Please describe the problem in at least 10 characters."]
      }
    }
  }
  ```
}
